virglrenderer: AV1 film grain pictures also go into the buffer the guest shows

What: an AV1 picture whose description names a film grain target (Mesa sends
one only with apply_grain) is delivered into that buffer too, not only into the
decode target: the same picture (VideoToolbox applies the grain), the same
copy and edge fill, both marked pending until the picture is in (and cleared
when the guest destroys either). The backend keeps a list of its buffers to
find the target by the ID vrend_video.c puts into the description. One more
GPU copy per picture, only for AV1 with film grain.
Why: Chrome decodes an AV1 picture with film grain into its reconstruction
surface and shows another one, current_display_picture, which Mesa sends as
film_grain_target (picture_av1.c). The backend wrote only the decode target,
so on Macs that decode AV1 on the media engine (M3, M4) Chrome showed a buffer
the Mac never wrote for that picture: an older picture of its pool, or zeros
(green). VideoToolbox's picture is bit for bit dav1d's decode with the grain.
Upstream: this backend is OmacVM's; virglrenderer's own VA-API backend passes
film_grain_target to the host's VA-API. Not sent.
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-video-padding.c, the av1fg-320x180.ivf case (SVT-AV1
with film grain, decoded into one buffer, shown from another as Mesa sends
Chrome's pictures): the shown buffer must hold dav1d's picture with the grain
bit for bit and its padding the picture's edge; before, it kept the marker in
every texel. Runs with the GPU and CPU copy, the fill off and
OMACVM_VIDEO_DECODE_SYNC=1.
